    <statement><p>Find the exact length of the polar curve
<me>r=3\sin(\theta),\;\;0 \le \theta \le \pi/3.</me></p>
<p>Length = <fillin characters="45" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>3.14159265358979</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>SOLUTION</p>
<p>The arc length for a polar curve is given by <m>{\displaystyle \int_{a}^{b}}\, \sqrt{r^2+\left(\frac{dr}{d\theta}\right)^2 }\,d\theta</m>.</p>
<p>So for  <m>r= 3\sin(\theta)</m> on the interval <m>0 \le \theta \le \frac{\pi}{3}</m>, we have</p>
<p><me>\begin{array}{rcl} 
L \amp =\amp  
{\displaystyle \int_{a}^{b}}\, \sqrt{r^2+\left(\frac{dr}{d\theta}\right)^2 }\,d\theta  \\
\amp =\amp {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{\pi/3}\, \sqrt{\left(3\sin(\theta)\right)^2+\left(-3\cos(\theta)\right)^2 }\,d\theta} \\  
\amp =\amp  {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{\pi/3}\, \sqrt{9\sin^2(\theta)+9\cos^2(\theta)} \,d\theta}\\
\amp =\amp   {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{\pi/3}\, 3\,d\theta } =  \Big.3\theta\Big|_{0}^{\pi/3} \\ 
\amp =\amp   3\left(\frac{\pi}{3}\right)-0=\pi\\
\end{array}</me></p>
</solution>
